WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.740
If the Merkel cell holds the press, you might expect its own current to last.

2
00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:09.620
Does it?

3
00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:14.700
In cultured Merkel cells from mouse pups, pressing evokes a tall, brief current.

4
00:00:15.683 --> 00:00:20.023
It inactivates within milliseconds, then settles to a small steady current.

5
00:00:21.033 --> 00:00:24.073
The steady part is only about one twentieth of the peak.

6
00:00:25.067 --> 00:00:27.587
So how can a mostly brief current hold a press?

7
00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:30.140
Compare charge, not peaks.

8
00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:32.940
Charge is the area under the current trace.

9
00:00:33.983 --> 00:00:38.403
Predict. Suppose the steady part lasts through a one-second hold.

10
00:00:39.303 --> 00:00:42.523
Which part brings in more charge: the tall peak, or the steady part?

11
00:00:48.483 --> 00:00:50.203
The small steady part, by far.

12
00:00:51.083 --> 00:00:54.463
In this thought experiment, it carries nearly nine tenths of the charge.

13
00:00:55.516 --> 00:00:58.476
The peak's charge is all in within tens of milliseconds.

14
00:00:59.516 --> 00:01:02.256
A steady current keeps adding charge for as long as it flows.

15
00:01:03.350 --> 00:01:04.830
Peaks mislead about holds.

16
00:01:05.833 --> 00:01:08.813
But the membrane leaks, so the brief charge drains away.

17
00:01:09.673 --> 00:01:12.553
A steady current holds the cell depolarized while it flows.

18
00:01:13.666 --> 00:01:18.106
The authors proposed that voltage-gated calcium channels amplify it.

19
00:01:18.666 --> 00:01:20.346
That is a proposal, not proven.

20
00:01:21.416 --> 00:01:24.436
There is a second clue, in the timing of the afferent's spikes.

21
00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:27.400
Irregularity can be measured.

22
00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:31.920
Divide the standard deviation of the gaps between spikes by their mean.

23
00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:38.930
Predict. In a noise-free integrate-and-fire model with a fixed threshold and reset,

24
00:01:39.550 --> 00:01:43.110
a steady current above threshold makes it fire like a metronome.

25
00:01:44.050 --> 00:01:46.910
What could randomly timed packets of transmitter do instead?

26
00:01:52.933 --> 00:01:58.273
One possible result is irregular firing, depending on the timing and size of the

27
00:01:58.273 --> 00:02:02.313
packets. And light on the Merkel cells gave an irregular hold.

28
00:02:03.316 --> 00:02:04.756
That is a clue, not proof.

29
00:02:05.656 --> 00:02:09.836
A branched ending with several spike sites could add irregularity of its own.

30
00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:12.420
So what crosses that synapse?

31
00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:14.160
That is still argued.

32
00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:20.040
In mouse touch domes, the evidence points toward noradrenaline on beta β2 receptors.

33
00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:22.600
The channel it opens is unknown.

34
00:02:23.683 --> 00:02:29.503
In rodent whiskers, fast synaptic currents were attributed to proton-gated ASIC

35
00:02:29.503 --> 00:02:30.503
channels instead.

36
00:02:31.533 --> 00:02:34.433
Neither account is settled, and neither is refuted.

37
00:02:35.516 --> 00:02:38.736
Keep the idea. The ending feels the change.

38
00:02:39.636 --> 00:02:41.036
The Merkel cell holds the press.

39
00:02:41.916 --> 00:02:43.176
How it signals is still open.
